  lessons: [
  {t:"What the CRS Is, and What It Sits On", d:"Designation vs. license, RRC and NAR membership", len:"14 min",
   script:"Start with the thing most study guides skip. The Certified Residential Specialist is a designation, not a license. It gives you no authority to list, sell, or represent anybody. Underneath it you must already hold an active real estate license issued by your state real estate commission, and you must be a dues-paying member of the Residential Real Estate Council, which is an affiliate of the National Association of REALTORS. So the order is always the same. State license first. R R C membership second. C R S third. If you are still working through your pre-license hours, the state module at the end of this track is where you should be spending your time."},
  {t:"The Three Paths to the Designation", d:"Designation, Pro Program and Managers paths", len:"18 min",
   script:"The Residential Real Estate Council revised the requirements, and older material is now simply wrong. There are three paths. The standard Designation path asks for thirty R R C education credits plus sixty closed transactions, or thirty million dollars in volume, across the last five years. The Pro Program path is for agents with at least ten years in residential real estate and asks for sixteen R R C credits plus a hundred and fifty transactions, or an average of one million dollars a year with at least forty transactions. The Managers path asks for forty-eight credits and four years of management, or four hundred closed transactions overseen, or eighty million dollars overseen. There is no exam on any path."},
  {t:"Listing and Seller Representation", d:"Listing presentation, marketing, net sheets", len:"24 min",
   script:"The listing side is the heart of the C R S curriculum. You are being trained to win the listing and then to earn it. That means a listing presentation built on a defensible comparative market analysis, a written marketing plan the seller can hold you to, and a net sheet that shows the seller what actually lands in their pocket after payoff, commission, transfer taxes, and prorations. Two things separate a specialist here. First, you price to the market rather than to the seller's hope. Second, you set the price-reduction conversation up at the listing appointment, before you need it, so it is a scheduled review and not an argument."},
  {t:"Buyer Representation After the 2024 Settlement", d:"Written buyer agreements, compensation, fiduciary duty", len:"26 min",
   script:"Buyer representation changed in a way that older training does not reflect. Under the National Association of REALTORS settlement practice changes that took effect in August of twenty twenty-four, a REALTOR working with a buyer must have a written buyer agreement signed before touring a home, and that agreement must state the compensation the agent will receive in a specific and objective amount. It cannot be open-ended. Offers of compensation to buyer brokers can no longer be published on a Multiple Listing Service, although compensation itself is still negotiable and can still be paid by a seller off the M L S. Your fiduciary duties have not changed. What changed is that the paperwork now has to come first."},
  {t:"Pricing Strategy and Market Analysis", d:"The three approaches to value, CMAs, appraisal gaps", len:"20 min",
   script:"Pricing is its own discipline, which is why the National Association of REALTORS made it a separate certification called Pricing Strategy Advisor. There are three approaches to value. Sales comparison is the workhorse for residential. Cost is for new construction and unusual properties. Income is for rentals and commercial. A comparative market analysis is your opinion of market value, not a certified appraisal, and the difference matters when a lender's appraiser comes in low. When that happens you have three moves: ask the buyer to cover the gap in cash, request a reconsideration of value with better comparables, or renegotiate the price."},
  {t:"Your State Real Estate License", d:"State-specific — updates automatically per state", len:"22 min", stateSpecific:true,
   script:null},
  ],

